n the final  statement or communique that will be  issued tomorrow when the summit wraps up  pharah  david aiken in rwanda thank you david  ukrainian troops withdraw from a key  battleground ahead the impact this could  have on russia's war  you're watching global national  for weeks ukrainian forces have fought  to hold on to a besieged eastern city  but today keeve has ordered its troops  to withdraw from severe doneesk  [Music]  the retreat is a significant victory for  russia as it aims to take over the  entire donbass region ukraine says  moscow's next target is the neighboring  city of lisensk which would give the  kremlin full control over luhansk's one  of two regions that make up donbass  and here in canada students are heading  to summer vacation and they know that  they'll be back in school in the fall  but in ukraine that's not the case the  school year was cut short when russia  invaded in february and as global's sean  o'shea reports the combination of the  war and the pandemic have made for a  terrible school year  outside this downtown kharkiv school  it's unusually quiet but in a more  dangerous part of this ukrainian city  bombarded by russia  you can hear distant artillery outside  another school what's missing in these  pictures the obvious there are no  children  well canadian kids are thinking about  summer because school's coming to an end  the physical school came to an end long  ago here when the war began in ukraine  this is the backyard at one of the  schools we're at people are living in  the basement people can't come to the  school it's not safe to play in the  playground and the question is when will  it ever be safe again  school director alexander grayanik takes  global news on a tour first stop  downstairs where we meet someone who's  made the school her refuge are you  afraid when you hear those sounds  certainly certainly and all of us  i'm afraid  but is it preferable to be here than  back to where you live yes you're right  irina isn't just sheltering at the  school she's a teacher here for the last  40 years i know this school  when the war began schools here made a  switch in a way canadians know very well  what happened to their school year when  all this happened  [Music]  children stayed home he says they  transitioned to remote learning although  with internet speeds that are relatively  slow in many areas that's a big  challenge at another nearby school you  can see why students aren't able to  study together this school was attacked  in april likely hit by russian rockets  in the middle of the night the building  is a wreck this classroom took one of  the worst hits when the explosions  happened and perhaps in a mark of irony  this classroom was used to teach russian  literature  but in another room the complete  opposite result a room designed as a  shrine to ukrainian culture wasn't  damaged at all the dresses dolls and  other pieces all perfectly intact  including this gryanic's prized  contribution to the collection his  grandfather's 100 year old milking stool  as for the kids the world bank estimates  ukrainian children have lost more than a  year of learning between the war and the  pandemic and it'll cost them 10 percent  per year in future earnings but right  now the issue at hand is getting kids to  school somehow peace would make all the  difference what do you hope happens next  year for the next school year we can't  hope we wait  we wait we don't know  i would hope  waiting to see what back to school looks  like this fall  shawn o'shea global news kharkiv  returns next toronto gears up for the  return of one of the largest pride  celebrations in the world  with the sixth pick in the 2022 nba  draft  the indiana pacers select  benedict matherin  from montreal  [Applause]  a big night for canada is montreal's  benedict matherin was the first of two  canucks taken back to back in the first  round of last night's nba draft  nineteen-year-old shayden sharp from  london ontario went to the portland  trailblazers with the seventh overall  pick and it is only the second time in  history two canadians have gone in the  top 10 of the draft  now after two long years toronto's pride  parade is once again taken to the  streets pride toronto estimates that  nearly two million people are going to  be out for this weekend's celebrations  of welcome change of course for both  participants and businesses in the city  mike trolley reports  bathed in a sea of rainbows at toronto's  gay village is basking in pride's happy  raise  that's where we found nick bobbing his  head to bollywood music what are you  expecting this year  lots of dancing hopefully making outfit  strangers  toronto has one of the largest pride  festivals in the world a staggering 1.7  million people are expected to take in  sunday's parade which itself will have  over 35 000 marchers  organizers tried their best to make the  virtual pride parties of the past two  years fun  and we're all gonna dance together hit  it but nothing compares to the real  thing downtown businesses should be  happy as well with every hotel room in  the city booked  these three friends just arrived from  san diego we came last night just to  hang out and check out the places and  it's very very diverse around here it's  really cool for all the glow within the  pride bubble organizers can't ignore the  real world around them and the recent  increase in anti-lgbt crime there have  been no direct threats to pride but  organizers aren't taking any chances so  they've increased security at all events  most of the attacks are nothing the  community hasn't seen before slurs flags  torn down but canada's anti-hate network  warns of an increase in threats to those  doing lgbt advocacy while concerning the  people we spoke with say they don't  expect that to change  honestly and i've told many people i  appreciate that people go online and  defend us and defend our community and  that we do ourselves but at some 